  "answer_text": "Typical linebacker jersey numbers depend on the level of play (NFL, college, or high school) and whether you are looking at the **modern era** or **traditional football history**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. NFL (National Football League)\nThe NFL significantly loosened its jersey number rules recently, giving linebackers a much wider selection.\n\n#### **Modern NFL Rules (2023\u2013Present)**\nToday, NFL linebackers are allowed to wear:\n*   **0\u201359** and **90\u201399**\n\nBecause of this rule change (which began in 2021 and was updated to include the number \"0\" in 2023), many star modern linebackers wear single-digit numbers or other non-traditional numbers:\n*   **Roquan Smith** (#0)\n*   **Patrick Queen** (#6)\n*   **Micah Parsons** (#11)\n*   **Fred Warner** (#54 \u2013 stuck with a traditional number)\n\n#### **The \"Classic\" NFL Era (1973\u20132020)**\nFor nearly 50 years, the NFL had very strict numbering rules to help officials quickly identify positions. During this era, linebackers were highly restricted:\n*   **50\u201359** (The most traditional linebacker range)\n*   **90\u201399** (Added in 1984 to accommodate teams running 3-4 defenses who were running out of numbers in the 50s)\n\nAlmost every legendary, old-school linebacker wore a number in this range:\n*   **Ray Lewis** (#52)\n*   **Brian Urlacher** (#54)\n*   **Mike Singletary** (#55)\n*   **Junior Seau** (#55)\n*   **Lawrence Taylor** (#56)\n*   **Von Miller** (#58)\n*   **T.J. Watt** (#90)\n\n---\n\n### 2. College Football (NCAA)\nCollege football has always been much more relaxed with defensive numbers than the NFL.\n\n*   **Allowed Numbers:** **0\u201399**\n*   **The Rule:** Unlike offensive players (where offensive linemen *must* wear 50\u201379), there are no positional restrictions for defensive players in college. \n*   **Typical Numbers:** College linebackers frequently wear single-digits (**1, 3, 5, 9, 11**) because they are highly coveted status symbols on campus. However, traditional linebacker numbers in the 30s, 40s, and 50s remain very popular.\n*   **USC\u2019s Famous No. 55:** The University of Southern California (USC) has a legendary tradition where the **#55 jersey** is only given to the team's most dominant, elite linebacker (worn by Junior Seau, Willie McGinest, Chris Claiborne, and Keith Rivers).\n\n---\n\n### 3. High School Football (NFHS)\nLike college football, high school rules (governed by the NFHS) do not restrict defensive players to specific number ranges.\n\n*   **Allowed Numbers:** **0\u201399** (NFHS approved the use of the number 0 starting in the 2024 season).\n*   **Typical Numbers:** High school linebackers can wear almost any number, though many coaches still prefer to assign them numbers in the **40s, 50s, or 90s** to maintain a traditional look or because smaller numbers are saved for wide receivers, running backs, and defensive backs.",
